'''Jonathan Septer Kreiss-Tomkins''' (born February 7, 1989) is an American politician who served as a member of the [[Alaska House of Representatives]] from 2013 to 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |title= |url=https://voteref.com/VoterDetails?personId=78e413a0-7248-4691-969d-e22bd29f4d23&state=AK&search=jonathan+kreiss-tomkins |url-status=live |access-date=2026-10-27 |website=Voteref}}</ref> A [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]], he represented the state's 35th district, which encompasses many Southeast island communities, including [[Hoonah, Alaska|Hoonah]], [[Sitka, Alaska|Sitka]], [[Kake, Alaska|Kake]], [[Klawock, Alaska|Klawock]], [[Craig, Alaska|Craig]], [[Angoon, Alaska|Angoon]] and [[Petersburg, Alaska|Petersburg]]. Kreiss-Tomkins is a candidate in the [[2026 Alaska gubernatorial election]].

== Early life and education ==
Kreiss-Tomkins was born on February 7, 1989, in [[Sitka, Alaska]].<ref>{{Cite web |title= Alaska State Legislature |url= https://www.akleg.gov/basis/Member/Detail/32?code=KRE |access-date= 2026-10-09 |website= www.akleg.gov}}</ref> His mother, Connie Kreiss, worked as a [[family doctor]].<ref>{{Cite web |last= Woolsey |first= Robert |date= 2013-05-08 |title= The Nation: Kreiss-Tomkins a 'Lesson For the Left' |url= https://alaskapublic.org/news/2013-05-08/the-nation-kreiss-tomkins-a-lesson-for-the-left |access-date= 2026-10-09 |website= Alaska Public Media |language= en}}</ref> He graduated from [[Sitka High School]] as an [[Eagle Scout]]. He attended [[Yale University]] but dropped out to run for the [[Alaska House of Representatives]] in 2012.<ref>{{Cite web |title= Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins |url=https://leadershipnowproject.org/candidates/jonathan-kreiss-tomkins |access-date= 2026-10-09 |website= Leadership Now Project |language= en-US}}</ref>

==Alaska House of Representatives==
===2012 election===
[[File:Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins and Terry Gardiner.jpg|thumb|Kreiss-Tomkins and [[Terry Gardiner]] during a panel discussion at the [[University of Alaska Fairbanks]] in March 2013.<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Woolsey|first1=Robert|title=Kreiss-Tomkins: Not Alaska's youngest lawmaker| url=https://www.kcaw.org/2012/12/06/kreiss-tomkins-not-alaskas-youngest-lawmaker|access-date=June 11, 2022|work=[[KCAW|Raven Radio]]|date=December 6, 2012}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last1=Woolsey|first1=Robert|title=Alaska's youngest legislator recalls first day in office| url=https://www.kcaw.org/2012/12/27/alaskas-youngest-legislator-recalls-first-day-in-office|access-date=June 11, 2022|work=Raven Radio|date=December 27, 2012}}</ref>]]
Due to the [[2010 United States Census|2010 census]] [[redistricting]], [[Bill Thomas (Alaska politician)|Bill Thomas]], a state representative since 2004, was redrawn into a slightly altered district. Kreiss-Tomkins's hometown of [[Sitka, Alaska|Sitka]] fell into the 34th district, and no other Democratic candidates filed to run in the primary, so he decided to run for the seat.<ref>{{cite news |title= Sitkan Enters House Race in New District |url= http://sitkasentinel.com/7/component/content/article?id=4688 |access-date= 31 January 2013| newspaper= Sitka Sentinel}}</ref> Kreiss-Tomkins dropped out of Yale University after three years to run.<ref>{{Cite news| url= https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2018/01/12/how-to-turn-red-state-blue-purple-alaska-politics-2018-216304/ |title= How to Turn a Red State Purple (Democrats Not Required)| work= POLITICO Magazine| access-date= 2018-01-12}}</ref>

The race was very close, and Kreiss-Tomkins won with 50.12% of the vote. After a recount that decreased his margin of victory from 34 votes to 32, the race was called on December 3.<ref>{{cite web |title= House District 34 Recount - December 3, 2012 |url= https://www.elections.alaska.gov/results/12GENR/data/Results-HD34-Recount.pdf |publisher= Alaska Division of Elections |access-date= 31 January 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= Miller| first= Mark |title= Kreiss-Tomkins wins seat by 32 votes |url=http://juneauempire.com/state/2012-12-03/kreiss-tomkins-wins-seat-32-votes |access-date= 31 January 2013 |newspaper= Juneau Empire |date= December 4, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151203124244/http://juneauempire.com/state/2012-12-03/kreiss-tomkins-wins-seat-32-votes| archive-date= December 3, 2015 |url-status= dead}}</ref>

===2014 election===
[[File:Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins at Alaska Day Parade crop.jpg|thumb|Kreiss-Tomkins, campaigning for reelection in October 2014 during the [[Alaska Day|Alaska Day Parade]].]]
In the 2014 midterm elections, Kreiss-Tomkins was reelected with 60% of the vote (3,393 votes to 2,288).<ref>{{cite web |title= Kreiss-Tomkins reelected by wide margin |date= November 5, 2014 |url= http://www.kcaw.org/2014/11/05/kreiss-tomkins-reelected-by-wide-margin/ |publisher= KCAW |access-date= 10 November 2014}}</ref> His opponent was Petersburg Republican Steven Samuelson, who had lost twice before to Peggy Wilson of Wrangell in primaries. Kreiss-Tomkins was elected in a slightly altered district (renumbered House District 35) that now included Petersburg and the northern end of Prince of Wales Island but no longer covered Haines or Metlakatla.<ref>{{cite web |title= No primary challengers for Sitka house seat |date= June 9, 2014 |url= http://www.kcaw.org/2014/06/09/no-primary-challengers-for-central-southeast-house-seat/ |publisher= KCAW |access-date= 10 November 2014}}</ref>

