===Legislation===
Kreiss-Tomkins introduced House Bill 216, making 20 Alaska Native languages official state languages. The bill was passed into law in April 2014.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Chappell |first1=Bill |title=Alaska OKs Bill Making Native Languages Official |url=https://www.npr.org/sections/thetwo-way/2014/04/21/305688602/alaska-oks-bill-making-native-languages-official |access-date=30 September 2026 |publisher=NPR |date=April 21, 2014}}</ref>

==2026 gubernatorial campaign==
{{main|2026 Alaska gubernatorial election}}

On February 4, 2026, Kreiss-Tomkins announced his candidacy for [[governor of Alaska]] in [[2026 Alaska gubernatorial election|2026]].<ref name="Candidacy">{{cite news |last1= Brooks |first1= James |title= Alaska's race for governor picks up 16th candidate, a former state legislator from Sitka |url= https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/02/04/alaskas-governor-race-picks-up-16th-candidate-a-former-state-legislator-from-sitka/ |access-date= February 4, 2026 |work=[[Alaska Beacon]] |date= February 4, 2026}}</ref>

Kreiss-Tomkins spent the most money of any candidate during the primary campaign and reported the largest number of individual Alaskan contributors, but raised most of his money from donors outside Alaska, including employees of [[Anthropic]] and others tied to the [[artificial intelligence]] industry.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Samuels |first= Iris |title= Democrat in Alaska's governor race reports $1.8 million fundraising haul, driven by Californians |url= https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/07/23/democrat-in-alaskas-governor-race-reports-18-million-fundraising-haul-driven-by-californians/ |access-date= 2026-10-20 |website= Anchorage Daily News}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1= Samuels |first1= Iris |title= In Alaska's crowded race for governor, candidates struggle to stand out as primary nears |url= https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/08/in-alaskas-crowded-race-for-governor-candidates-struggle-to-stand-out/ |access-date= August 29, 2026 |work= [[Anchorage Daily News]] |date= August 9, 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260810164447/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/08/in-alaskas-crowded-race-for-governor-candidates-struggle-to-stand-out/ |archive-date= August 10, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1= Samuels |first1= Iris |title= Candidates in Alaska's open governor's race spend big ahead of Tuesday's primary |url= https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/12/candidates-in-alaskas-open-governors-race-spend-big-ahead-of-tuesdays-primary/ |access-date= August 29, 2026 |work= [[Anchorage Daily News]] |date= August 13, 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260814183245/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/12/candidates-in-alaskas-open-governors-race-spend-big-ahead-of-tuesdays-primary/ |archive-date= August 14, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref>

Kreiss-Tomkins placed first in the [[nonpartisan primary#Alaska|nonpartisan primary]] with 22.5% of the vote, ahead of fellow Democrat [[Tom Begich]] with 20.8%. Begich subsequently withdrew from the election and endorsed Kreiss-Tomkins.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Samuels |first1= Iris |title= Tom Begich says he will withdraw from Alaska governor's race |url= https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/28/tom-begich-says-he-will-withdraw-from-alaska-governors-race/ |access-date =August 29, 2026 |work= [[Anchorage Daily News]] |date= August 28, 2026}}</ref>

==Personal life==
As a freshman at [[Sitka High School]] in 2003, Kreiss-Tomkins attracted national attention as a major online organizer for [[Howard Dean]]'s presidential campaign.<ref>{{cite news| last= Miller| first= Andrew |title= Teen is top cyber fan of presidential candidate |url= http://juneauempire.com/stories/111703/sta_cyberfan.shtml |access-date= 2 February 2013 |newspaper= Sitka Sentinel| date= 17 November 2003}}</ref>

He is a long-distance runner, winning the Alpine Adventure Race in 2009<ref>{{cite web |title= Alpine Adventure Run Preview |url= http://sitkasentinel.com/7/component/content/article?id=4797 |publisher= Sitka Sentinel |access-date= 30 January 2013}}</ref> and placing second in the Coyote Two Moon ultramarathon in 2010.<ref>{{cite news |last= Beckett |first= Sean |title= The ultramarathon: if a marathon just isn't enough |url= http://yaleherald.com/sports/the-ultramarathon-if-a-marathon-just-isn%E2%80%99t-enough/ |access-date= 3 February 2013 |newspaper= Yale Herald |date= 3 April 2010}}</ref> While attending Yale, Kreiss-Tomkins was organizer for Harriers of the Night, a running club whose members met weekly at midnight to run from the Yale [[Women's Table]] to [[East Rock]] and back.

He is also a mountaineer and in 2009 he climbed the highest volcano in the world, Argentina's [[Ojos del Salado]], to conclusively measure its height against a neighboring peak in Chile.<ref>{{cite web |title= The Year in Sitka Sports |url= http://sitkasentinel.com/7/component/content/article?id=4189 |publisher= Sitka Sentinel |access-date= 2 February 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last= Gonzalez |first= Susan |title= Yale Student Makes Mission Measuring Mountains |date= February 25, 2011 |url= http://news.yale.edu/2011/02/25/spotlight-yale-student-makes-mission-measuring-mountains |publisher= Yale News |access-date= 30 January 2013}}</ref>

Kreiss-Tomkins founded [[Outer Coast College]]<ref>{{cite news |last= Worthen| first= Molly |title= The Anti-College Is on the Rise |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2019/06/08/opinion/sunday/college-anti-college-mainstream-universities.html |date= 8 June 2019 |access-date= 5 January 2020 |newspaper=The New York Times}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title= An alternative college education |url= https://www.economist.com/christmas-specials/2017/12/19/an-alternative-college-education |access-date= 5 January 2020 |publisher= The Economist |date= 19 December 2017}}</ref> and the Alaska Fellows Program.<ref>{{cite news |last= Campbell |first= Aidan |title= Anchored in Alaska |url= https://yaledailynews.com/blog/2019/03/05/anchored-in-alaska/ |access-date= 5 January 2020 |newspaper= Yale Daily News |date= 5 March 2019}}</ref> In 2020, he co-founded [[Covid Act Now]].<ref>{{cite web |last= Robertson |first= Adi |title= The Best Graphs And Data For Tracking The Coronavirus Pandemic |url= https://www.theverge.com/2020/4/2/21201832/novel-coronavirus-covid-19-best-graphs-tracking-data |access-date= 28 March 2022 |website= The Verge |date= 2 April 2020}}</ref>

